PART A4 -- hull dimensions, brute force over all 4^3 = 64 vectors
==============================================================================
  k  form        dim hull_k(C)   dim hull_k(phiC)  changed?
  0  Euclidean   0               1                 YES
  1  Hermitian   0               0                 no

  hull_0 of C (as vectors): [('0', '0', '0')]
  hull_0 of phi(C) (as vectors): [('0', '0', '0'), ('1', 'w^2', 'w'), ('w', '1', 'w^2'), ('w^2', 'w', '1')]

==============================================================================
PART A5 -- the criterion a_i^(1+p^k) constant, checked on this phi
==============================================================================
  a = (a_0,a_1,a_2) = ('1', 'w', 'w^2')
  k=0: a_i^(1+2^0) = ('1', 'w^2', 'w')   constant: False   => phi is NOT a similarity of the k-Galois form
  k=1: a_i^(1+2^1) = ('1', '1', '1')   constant: True   => phi IS a similarity of the k-Galois form

  prediction: hull_0 may change (phi not a similarity),
              hull_1 must NOT change (phi IS a similarity).
  observed:    hull_0 0 -> 1;  hull_1 0 -> 0

==============================================================================
PART A6 -- verification of the criterion identity on random data
==============================================================================
  <phi u, phi v>_k  ==  sum_i a_i^(1+p^k) u_i v_i^(p^k)
  trials: 300;  mismatches: 0

==============================================================================
PART B -- a k=1 (Hermitian) counterexample over F_9
==============================================================================
  Why F_4 cannot give one: for q=4, k=1, a^(1+p) = a^3 = 1 for every
  nonzero a, so a_i^(1+p) is always constant and EVERY monomial map is a
  Hermitian similarity.  Over F_9, a^(1+3) = Norm(a) is a nontrivial
  character F_9^* -> F_3^*, so non-constant diagonals exist.

  F_9 = F_3(w) with w^2 + 2w + 2 = 0; elements encoded 0..8
  Norm(a) = a^(1+3) = a^4 in F_3: 1:1, 2:1, 3:2, 4:1, 5:2, 6:2, 7:2, 8:1

  n = 5; units mod n = [1, 2, 3, 4]
  (b, s, code) triples checked: 256
  Hermitian hull changes      : 32

  FIRST WITNESS
    source ring R_a = F_9[x]/(x^5 - 4)   [a = b^s with b = 3, s = 2]
    generator g = [2, 6, 1]   dim = 3
    map psi_s: f(x) -> f(x^2),  diagonal scalings b^floor(si/n)
    image is a 3-constacyclic code, same dimension
    dim_F9 hull_1 :  2  ->  0
    diagonal scalings a_i = [1, 1, 1, 3, 3]
    a_i^(1+3) = [1, 1, 1, 2, 2]  (non-constant -> not a similarity)

==============================================================================
SUMMARY OF TASK A
==============================================================================
  C = <1+x> in F_4[x]/(x^3-1):            dim 2, |C| = 16
  phi = diag(1,w,w^2) = f(x)->f(wx):      phi(C) cyclic, dim 2, |phi(C)| = 16
  weight distributions identical:         True
  hull_0 (Euclidean):  0 -> 1   CHANGED
  hull_1 (Hermitian):  0 -> 0   NOT changed

  The Euclidean counterexample stands.
  The Hermitian case is NOT a counterexample at q=4, and cannot be:
  over F_4 every nonzero a has a^3 = 1, so a_i^(1+p) = 1 for all a_i and
  EVERY monomial map is a similarity of the Hermitian form.
  Hermitian counterexamples found over F_9: 32.

